{"shortDescription":"RNS Number : 9058S  Zopa Group PLC  01 September 2026   Zopa Group PLC: H1 2026 Trading UpdateToday, Zopa Group PLC has provided an unaudited update on its consolidated financial performance for H1 2026 (the six months ended 30 June 2026).Key Highlights·      In H1 2026, we continued to scale the H…","astDescription":{"type":"root","children":["RNS Number : 9058S  Zopa Group PLC  01 September 2026   ",{"type":"p","children":["Zopa Group PLC: H1 2026 Trading Update"]},{"type":"p","children":["Today, Zopa Group PLC has provided an unaudited update on its consolidated financial performance for H1 2026 (the six months ended 30 June 2026)."]},{"type":"p","children":["Key Highlights"]},{"type":"p","children":["·      In H1 2026, we continued to scale the Home of Money across everyday banking, borrowing, spending, payments and wealth:"]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Total customers reached 1.9m (H1 2025: 1.5m), up 26% year-on-year (YoY), with one in three now holding more than one product."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Gross new lending increased 35% YoY to £1.6bn (H1 2025: £1.2bn), led by unsecured personal loans (UPL), with growth increasingly being driven organically through cross-sell and re-borrowing. New lending growth led to average gross loan balances of £3.98bn for H1 2026, up from £3.24bn in H1 2025, an increase of 22%."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Revenue rose 20% to £212m (H1 2025: £176m), supported by growth in customer numbers, loan balances and product engagement."]},{"type":"p","children":["·      Net interest margin, which includes all interest-bearing assets, increased to 5.9% (H1 2025: 5.2%), as the proportion of excess liquid assets reduced. Excluding this impact, lending net interest margin, which focuses on lending assets, reduced to more normalised levels at 9.5% (H1 2025: 9.9%)."]},{"type":"p","children":["·      The macroeconomic outlook remains uncertain in the UK, particularly following the US / Iran conflict."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  The UK unemployment rate was higher in H1 2026 than H1 2025 and the outlook for near term future unemployment rates was worse at 30th June 2026 than at 30th June 2025."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Inflation expectations have also worsened and as a result interest rates are expected to be higher for longer."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  These factors are considered in our macro forecasting and hence IFRS9 impairment charge."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Underlying portfolio performance has been strong with further portfolio maturation, hence despite this moderate deterioration in the UK economy, our cost of risk was broadly stable at 5.1% (H1 2025: 5.0%). The absolute increase in credit impairment charges was primarily driven by the 22% increase in average gross loans."]},{"type":"p","children":["·      In H1 2026, our cost-to-income ratio fell marginally to 37.0% (H1 2025: 37.5%) with operating expenses including marketing costs increasing 22% YoY. Operating costs at a product level reduced at a faster rate. However, this was partly offset by the following mix effects and impacts:"]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Current accounts were publicly launched in July 2025, hence current account customer numbers and operating costs were higher in H1 2026 than H1 2025. Marketing to support current account growth was also further accelerated in H1 2026."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Additionally, H1 2026 includes a full six months of RVVUP operating costs following the acquisition of the business in August 2025."]},{"type":"p","children":["o  Both the current account and RVVUP investments are expected to support long-term business growth and diversification to enable further acceleration of revenue and profit trajectory in the medium term."]},{"type":"p","children":["·      Overall, our key KPI on profit before tax (excluding share-based payments and motor finance redress costs) remained flat at £20.1m in H1 2026, compared to £20.7m in H1 2025."]},{"type"